<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Humphrey returns four weeks after his first visit, four months after his radical cystoprostatectomy. For three weeks his appliance has leaked about twice a week, usually at night, and the skin under the lower edge of the wafer has become red and sore. He has had no fever, flank pain, or blood in the urine. He saw Dr. Kowalczyk of urology this morning with the wound, ostomy, and continence nurse, who measured the stoma and fitted a convex wafer with a barrier ring, stoma powder for the irritated skin, and a night drainage bag. Dr. Kowalczyk reviewed the pathology and agreed that he needs no further treatment, and set out the surveillance schedule. Erections have not returned since the operation; he understands why and does not want treatment now. He is back at full-time work, walks 20 to 30 minutes a day, and his appetite is good. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stoma</td><td>Leaks twice a week; sore peristomal skin; refitted today</td></tr><tr><td>Urinary</td><td>No fever, flank pain, or blood in the urine</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Urology and stoma nurse seen today</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Working full time; walking daily; weight up 0.7 kg</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

