  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bladder cancer after radical cystoprostatectomy, pT2a N0, no evidence of disease (Z85.51)</content>. Organ-confined disease with negative nodes and margins; no adjuvant therapy is indicated. Surveillance as set out by urology. [S]<list><item>CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis with a urographic phase at one year after surgery, then every 6 to 12 months to three years.</item><item>Renal ultrasound at six months after surgery.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ileal conduit urostomy with appliance leaks and moisture dermatitis (Z93.6)</content>. Leaks from a skin crease below the stoma when he sits; the irritation is from urine contact, not fungal. [S]<list><item>Convex wafer with a barrier ring, changed every three to four days; stoma powder sealed with a barrier film until the skin heals.</item><item>Night drainage bag.</item><item>Call the stoma nurse for any new leak.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">After cystectomy: kidney function and metabolic monitoring (Z90.6)</content>. Creatinine 1.12 mg/dL and bicarbonate 22 mmol/L at the first visit, the post-operative baseline. [S]<list><item>Chemistry at six months after surgery.</item><item>About 2 liters of fluid a day.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Chemistry with creatinine, chloride, and bicarbonate</td><td>Every 3 to 6 months in the first year after diversion, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Four weeks ago: creatinine 1.12 mg/dL, chloride 106, bicarbonate 22 mmol/L; next at six months after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Vitamin B12</td><td>Baseline, then yearly from two to three years after an ileal diversion</td><td>Up to date</td><td>412 pg/mL four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis (with urographic phase)</td><td>Every 6 to 12 months for 2 to 3 years after cystectomy, then yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>Pre-operative staging showed no spread; first surveillance scan at one year after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper-tract imaging (renal ultrasound or CT urography)</td><td>Every 6 months to year 3, then yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>Renal ultrasound at six months after surgery, in about two months</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Stoma and peristomal skin</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Moisture dermatitis below the stoma from leaks; appliance refitted by the stoma nurse</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Parastomal and incisional hernia</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress and depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Sexual function</td><td>After cystectomy and periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Erectile dysfunction since surgery; options explained by urology; no treatment wanted now</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Routine urine culture of conduit urine</td><td>Only for fever, flank pain, or other signs of infection</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Bacteria are expected in conduit urine; no symptoms today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urethral wash cytology</td><td>Periodically if the urethra is retained and at high risk</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Urethral margin negative and no prostatic urethral involvement</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adjuvant systemic therapy</td><td>Offered for pT3 to pT4 or node-positive disease</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>pT2a N0 with negative margins</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Urology and the wound, ostomy, and continence nurse</td><td>Once, then shared surveillance</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fluid intake and stoma self-care</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 2 liters a day; new appliance routine taught</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Leak-free urostomy appliance with healthy peristomal skin. In progress; target 2026-04-07 [S]</item><item>Stable kidney function (creatinine within 0.3 mg/dL of the post-operative baseline) and bicarbonate 22 mmol/L or more. In progress; target 2026-07-07 [S]</item><item>Regain stamina after surgery: walk 30 minutes a day and regain weight. In progress; target 2026-04-07 [S]</item><item>Written survivorship surveillance schedule in place, shared between urology and primary care. In progress; target 2026-07-07 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Holzbeierlein J, et al. Treatment of Non-Metastatic Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer: AUA/ASCO/SUO Guideline (2017; Amended 2020, 2024). J Urol 2024;212(1):3-10 (2024); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Bladder Cancer (2026); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, urologic cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Stoma nurse by telephone in one week. Primary care at six months after surgery with chemistry and a renal ultrasound. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-07: six-month post-cystectomy follow-up: kidney function, acid-base, upper-tract ultrasound, and review after a kidney infection. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
